---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import T from '../components/T.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Addressing & Routing"
  description="One subnet manager discovers every device, assigns every address and computes every forwarding table - and then adaptive routing overrules it, per packet, when a link congests."
  heading="Addressing and Routing"
  slug="/routing"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      Ethernet has no one in charge: switches learn what they can see and the network converges by
      agreement. An InfiniBand subnet has a single authority that knows the entire topology and
      writes every forwarding table in it. Almost everything distinctive about routing here
      follows from that one architectural difference.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sm">One thing in charge</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        Somewhere in the cluster a piece of software called the subnet manager is running. When
        the fabric powers up it goes looking: it finds every switch, every adapter and every cable
        between them, and builds a complete map.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then it hands out addresses and tells every switch exactly where to send traffic for every
        possible destination. A switch here does not work anything out for itself - it is given a
        table and follows it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The advantage is that somebody knows the whole shape of the network and can pick sensible
        routes through it. The cost is that this somebody now matters a great deal: while it is
        gone, traffic already flowing carries on, but nothing new can be worked out - a swapped
        cable or a replaced switch has nobody to tell the fabric about it.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        The <T id="subnet-manager">subnet manager</T> is "a centralized entity running on the
        server that discovers and configures all the InfiniBand fabric devices to enable traffic
        flow throughout the fabric".<Cite ids="nv-ufm" /> It assigns each port a
        <T id="lid">LID</T> - a subnet-local address, allocated rather than burned in, distinct
        from the permanent GUID - and computes a forwarding table for every switch, indexed by
        destination LID.
      </p>
      <p>
        So a route is a table lookup, not a computation. There is no learning, no flooding, no
        spanning tree and no convergence delay after a topology change, because nothing is
        discovered locally. The trade is that a topology change means the manager recomputes and
        redistributes tables, and the time that takes scales with the fabric.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        Centralised routing is viable here for a reason that does not generalise: an InfiniBand
        subnet is administratively singular. One owner, one physical site, a topology that is
        known in advance because somebody drew it, and a device count in the thousands rather than
        the billions. None of that is true of the internet, which is why the internet distributes
        its control plane and pays convergence costs instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it buys is worth the constraint. Because the manager knows the whole graph, it can
        compute routes that are deterministic and balanced by construction - and it can hand a
        topology-aware collectives library the actual topology rather than a guess. That is the
        precondition for both <a href="/topology#rails">rail alignment</a> and
        <a href="/sharp">in-network reduction</a>: neither works if nobody knows where anything is.
      </p>
    </L>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="Forwarding table explorer"
    hint="Hover a destination · click to keep it · then congest the link"
    caption="A four-leaf, two-spine fat tree with sixteen endpoints - the smallest arrangement that has a real routing choice in it. The static route picks a spine deterministically from the destination address, which is what makes it reproducible and also what makes it collide. Schematic: real forwarding tables are computed over the whole fabric, not from one field."
  >
    <div data-routing>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img"
        aria-label="A fat tree of sixteen endpoints, four leaf switches and two spine switches. Selecting a destination highlights the path the subnet manager's forwarding tables produce; congesting that path shows adaptive routing selecting the alternative spine.">
        <g data-fabric></g>
      </svg>
      <dl class="readout" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
        <div><dt>Source</dt><dd>endpoint 00</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Destination</dt><dd data-out="dst">-</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Path</dt><dd data-out="path">-</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Hops</dt><dd data-out="hops">-</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Adaptive routing</dt><dd data-out="ar">not engaged</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p data-out="verdict" style="margin:0.9rem 0 0;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--ink-2)"></p>
    </div>
    <div slot="controls" class="controls-row">
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Link state">
        <button type="button" data-congest="off" aria-pressed="true">Fabric clear</button>
        <button type="button" data-congest="on" aria-pressed="false">Congest the chosen uplink</button>
      </div>
      <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">Source is fixed at endpoint 00 so the choice of spine is the only thing changing.</span>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="adaptive">When the table is wrong</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        A fixed table has one weakness. If it always sends the same kind of traffic the same way,
        then when a lot of that traffic appears at once it all queues behind itself - while an
        equally good parallel route sits empty.
      </p>
      <p>
        Adaptive routing lets a switch break the rule when it sees that happening: same
        destination, different next hop, chosen because the prescribed one is busy.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        <T id="adaptive-routing">Adaptive routing</T> allows a switch to forward down any port that
        still reaches the destination, rather than only the one the table names. In a fat tree
        every upward port reaches everything, so the freedom is real and the choice can be made on
        occupancy.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cost is reordering. Packets of one flow now take different paths and can arrive out of
        order, which the transport has to tolerate - one of several places where RDMA's tight
        coupling to the fabric shows. On NVIDIA fabrics adaptive routing is part of the core
        subnet manager implementation rather than an add-on: since UFM v6.4 it no longer needs a
        separate plugin, and neither does <T id="shield">SHIELD</T>.<Cite ids="nv-ufm" />
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        Adaptive routing is not a nicety on a lossless fabric - it is load-bearing. On a dropping
        network a congested link degrades locally: the queue fills, packets are discarded, senders
        back off. On a lossless one the queue fills and <em>backpressure propagates</em>, so a
        single hotspot can stall senders that were never using the congested link at all. Moving
        flows off the hotspot is the only mechanism that stops that spread rather than merely
        bounding it, which is why <a href="/lossless#price">the previous chapter</a> ends by
        pointing here.
      </p>
      <p>
        SHIELD addresses the other failure mode. Recomputing fabric-wide forwarding tables takes
        time proportional to the fabric, which is far too slow to absorb a cable failure in the
        middle of a training run. SHIELD lets neighbouring switches agree a local detour
        immediately, so a link failure degrades a job rather than killing it, and the manager
        recomputes at its leisure.<Cite ids="nv-superpod-fabrics" />
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="key">
      <p>
        Deterministic routing gives you reproducibility and a topology-aware collectives library.
        Adaptive routing gives you resilience to the traffic that reproducibility cannot predict.
        The fabric runs both: the table is the plan, and adaptive routing is permission to deviate
        from it when the plan meets reality.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="myth">
      <p>
        <strong>"Adaptive routing makes topology irrelevant."</strong> It only ever chooses among
        paths that already exist. If a topology is oversubscribed at the upper tier, every
        alternative it can pick is oversubscribed too - it will spread the pain evenly and
        that is all. What the topology provides is the set of options;
        <a href="/topology">the next chapter</a> is about making that set large enough to matter.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Deeper title="Why LIDs are assigned rather than burned in">
      <p>
        Every port has a permanent GUID, the way an Ethernet interface has a MAC address. But
        switches do not forward on GUIDs - they forward on LIDs, which the subnet manager hands
        out at discovery time and which are meaningful only inside that subnet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reason is table size. A forwarding table indexed by an assigned, densely packed
        address space is a compact array; one indexed by 64-bit globally unique identifiers is a
        hash lookup over a sparse space. Assigning addresses is something you can only do if
        somebody is in charge of the whole subnet - so the centralised manager and the compact
        forwarding table are the same design decision, seen twice.
      </p>
    </Deeper>
  </div>

  <script>
    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-routing]');
    if (root) {
      const fig = root.closest('figure')!;
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const canvas = root.querySelector('[data-fabric]')!;
      const out = (k: string) => fig.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!;
      const el = (n: string, a: Record<string, string | number>) => {
        const e = document.createElementNS(NS, n);
        for (const k in a) e.setAttribute(k, String(a[k]));
        return e;
      };

      const ENDPOINTS = 16, PER_LEAF = 4, LEAVES = 4, SPINES = 2;
      const SRC = 0;
      const leafOf = (ep: number) => Math.floor(ep / PER_LEAF);
      /** The deterministic choice a forwarding table encodes: destination picks the spine. */
      const staticSpine = (dst: number) => dst % SPINES;

      const epX = (i: number) => 30 + i * 43;
      const leafX = (i: number) => 90 + i * 180;
      const spineX = (i: number) => 240 + i * 240;
      const EP_Y = 250, LEAF_Y = 150, SPINE_Y = 50;

      let pinned: number | null = null;
      let congested = false;

      const route = (dst: number) => {
        const sl = leafOf(SRC), dl = leafOf(dst);
        // Same leaf: the traffic never climbs, so there is no choice to make.
        if (sl === dl) return { spine: null, hops: 2, ar: false };
        const want = staticSpine(dst);
        // The congestion control congests whichever uplink the table chose, so
        // adaptive routing always has the other spine to move to.
        return { spine: congested ? (want + 1) % SPINES : want, hops: 4, ar: congested };
      };

      const render = (hover: number | null) => {
        const dst = hover ?? pinned;
        canvas.textContent = '';

        const r = dst === null ? null : route(dst);
        const sl = leafOf(SRC);
        const dl = dst === null ? null : leafOf(dst);

        // ── Links ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        for (let l = 0; l < LEAVES; l++) {
          for (let s = 0; s < SPINES; s++) {
            const onPath = r !== null && r.spine === s && (l === sl || l === dl);
            const isCongested = congested && l === sl && dst !== null && s === staticSpine(dst);
            canvas.appendChild(el('line', {
              x1: leafX(l), y1: LEAF_Y, x2: spineX(s), y2: SPINE_Y + 20,
              stroke: isCongested ? 'var(--danger)' : onPath ? 'var(--copper)' : 'var(--line-strong)',
              'stroke-width': onPath ? 3 : isCongested ? 3 : 1,
              'stroke-dasharray': isCongested ? '5 4' : 'none',
              opacity: onPath || isCongested ? 1 : 0.35,
            }));
          }
        }
        for (let e = 0; e < ENDPOINTS; e++) {
          const l = leafOf(e);
          const onPath = r !== null && (e === SRC || e === dst);
          canvas.appendChild(el('line', {
            x1: epX(e) + 14, y1: EP_Y, x2: leafX(l), y2: LEAF_Y + 20,
            stroke: onPath ? 'var(--copper)' : 'var(--line-strong)',
            'stroke-width': onPath ? 3 : 1, opacity: onPath ? 1 : 0.3,
          }));
        }

        // ── Switches ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
        for (let s = 0; s < SPINES; s++) {
          const on = r !== null && r.spine === s;
          canvas.appendChild(el('rect', { x: spineX(s) - 55, y: SPINE_Y, width: 110, height: 20,
            rx: 3, fill: on ? 'var(--switch)' : 'var(--line-strong)', opacity: on ? 0.95 : 0.4 }));
          const t = el('text', { x: spineX(s), y: SPINE_Y + 14, 'text-anchor': 'middle',
            fill: on ? '#fff' : 'var(--ink-3)' });
          t.textContent = `spine ${s}`;
          canvas.appendChild(t);
        }
        for (let l = 0; l < LEAVES; l++) {
          const on = r !== null && (l === sl || l === dl);
          canvas.appendChild(el('rect', { x: leafX(l) - 48, y: LEAF_Y, width: 96, height: 20,
            rx: 3, fill: on ? 'var(--cyan)' : 'var(--line-strong)', opacity: on ? 0.95 : 0.4 }));
          const t = el('text', { x: leafX(l), y: LEAF_Y + 14, 'text-anchor': 'middle',
            fill: on ? '#fff' : 'var(--ink-3)' });
          t.textContent = `leaf ${l}`;
          canvas.appendChild(t);
        }

        // ── Endpoints ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
        for (let e = 0; e < ENDPOINTS; e++) {
          const isSrc = e === SRC, isDst = e === dst;
          const g = el('g', { 'data-ep': e, tabindex: 0, role: 'button',
            'aria-label': `Destination endpoint ${String(e).padStart(2, '0')}` }) as SVGGElement;
          if (!isSrc) g.style.cursor = 'pointer';
          g.appendChild(el('rect', { x: epX(e), y: EP_Y, width: 28, height: 22, rx: 3,
            fill: isSrc ? 'var(--gpu)' : isDst ? 'var(--copper)' : 'var(--gpu)',
            opacity: isSrc || isDst ? 1 : 0.35 }));
          const t = el('text', { x: epX(e) + 14, y: EP_Y + 15, 'text-anchor': 'middle',
            fill: isSrc || isDst ? '#fff' : 'var(--ink-3)', 'font-size': 9 });
          t.textContent = String(e).padStart(2, '0');
          g.appendChild(t);
          canvas.appendChild(g);
        }

        const srcLabel = el('text', { x: epX(SRC) + 14, y: EP_Y + 38, 'text-anchor': 'middle',
          fill: 'var(--gpu)' });
        srcLabel.textContent = 'source';
        canvas.appendChild(srcLabel);

        wire();

        // ── Readouts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
        if (dst === null || r === null) {
          out('dst').textContent = '-';
          out('path').textContent = '-';
          out('hops').textContent = '-';
          out('ar').textContent = 'not engaged';
          out('verdict').textContent = 'Pick a destination. Endpoints 01 to 03 share a leaf switch with the source; everything from 04 upward has to climb to a spine and back down, which is where the routing decision lives.';
          return;
        }
        const name = String(dst).padStart(2, '0');
        out('dst').textContent = `endpoint ${name}${dst === pinned ? ' · pinned' : ''}`;
        out('path').textContent = r.spine === null
          ? `00 → leaf ${sl} → ${name}`
          : `00 → leaf ${sl} → spine ${r.spine} → leaf ${dl} → ${name}`;
        out('hops').textContent = String(r.hops);
        out('ar').textContent = r.ar ? 'engaged - rerouted' : 'not engaged';
        out('ar').style.color = r.ar ? 'var(--copper)' : '';

        out('verdict').textContent = r.spine === null
          ? `Same leaf switch, so the traffic never climbs the tree: two hops, no spine involved, and no routing choice to make. This is the case rail alignment is designed to manufacture - see the topology chapter.`
          : r.ar
            ? `The forwarding table sends destination ${name} via spine ${staticSpine(dst)}, but that uplink is congested, so adaptive routing has taken spine ${r.spine} instead. Same destination, same table, different port - chosen on occupancy at the moment the packet arrived.`
            : `The table sends destination ${name} via spine ${staticSpine(dst)}, deterministically, because the choice is a function of the destination address. Reproducible - and the reason several flows can pile onto one uplink while its neighbour sits idle.`;
      };

      const wire = () => {
        canvas.querySelectorAll<SVGGElement>('[data-ep]').forEach((g) => {
          const e = Number(g.dataset.ep);
          if (e === SRC) return;
          g.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => render(e));
          g.addEventListener('focus', () => render(e));
          g.addEventListener('click', () => {
            pinned = pinned === e ? null : e;
            render(pinned ?? e);
          });
          g.addEventListener('keydown', (ev) => {
            if (ev.key !== 'Enter' && ev.key !== ' ') return;
            ev.preventDefault();
            pinned = pinned === e ? null : e;
            render(pinned ?? e);
          });
        });
      };

      root.querySelector('svg')!.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => render(null));
      fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-congest]').forEach((b) => {
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          congested = b.dataset.congest === 'on';
          fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-congest]').forEach((o) =>
            o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === b)));
          render(null);
        });
      });

      render(null);
    }
  </script>
</Base>
